[preflight] snapshot 14 files from assets/ReCodeAgent/data/tool_projects/crust/aes128-simd/c
[delegate] analyst <- Explore the source codebase and produce a source map and brief that cites every module, identifying entry points, dependencies, and key abstractions.
[phase] Contract
[delegate] architect <- Produce the migration contract at analysis/brief.md based on analysis/source-map.md: define the target-language (Rust) crate layout, map every C module/function to its Rust equivalent, specify how the SSE intrinsics (g_mult_sse, Columns_sse, g_mult_sse_byte) will be handled (port to portable Rust or use std::arch), resolve the three functions declared in aes.h but never defined (drop, stub, or implement), and state the public API surface of the resulting crate. Cite every module from the source map.
[delegate] validator <- Validate the migration contract at analysis/brief.md against analysis/source-map.md and the actual C source files. Verify: (1) every C module and public function from the source map is accounted for in the contract's module mapping, (2) the 15 gap decisions are internally consistent and cite the correct source locations, (3) the target crate layout (9 src modules + 1 integration test) covers all mapped functions, (4) the 8 frozen contracts (block size, key size, round count, padding, SBOX/RSBOX/RCON values, GF polynomial, public API surface) match the actual C source, (5) the test strategy (1 translate + 8 characterize) is feasible given the source test file. Report any gaps, inconsistencies, or missing mappings.
[phase] Planning
[delegate] planner <- Read analysis/brief.md (the validated migration contract) and analysis/source-map.md. Produce the migration plan at meta/plan.md and the task list at meta/tasks.json that (1) partition all 9 target src modules plus the 1 integration test into an ordered set of batches, (2) order the batches bottom-up by dependency so each batch compiles against only already-migrated modules (e.g. cipher_utils/tables first, then keys, then matrix, then aes core, then padding/base64, then the public API facade, then the roundtrip test), (3) assign each batch a single translator task with a precise instruction naming the exact C source files and Rust target files to produce, and (4) include a final integration-test batch that runs the full Rust test suite. Cite the contract's module mapping and frozen contracts so the batches are traceable to analysis/brief.md.
[phase] Pilot
[delegate] translator <- Migrate batch-1 (tables/consts) per meta/plan.md and analysis/brief.md. Read source/src/aes.h (lines 10-12 for the Nb/Nk/Nr macros; lines 14-55 for the sbox, rsbox, and Rcon constant arrays). Produce two Rust files: src/tables.rs containing `pub const SBOX: [u8; 256]`, `pub const RSBOX: [u8; 256]`, and `pub const RCON: [u8; 11]` with values copied exactly from the C arrays (FIPS-197), and src/consts.rs containing `pub const NB: usize = 4`, `pub const NK: usize = 4`, `pub const NR: usize = 10`. Apply the contract's gap decisions: drop the `__attribute__((aligned(16)))` on the arrays, drop the `static const` header-guard pattern (use plain `pub const` in a module), and do not emit any C header guard. Do not use std::arch. Verify the SBOX/RSBOX/RCON values match FIPS-197 exactly and that NB=4, NK=4, NR=10 (frozen contracts). Ensure the module compiles standalone (no dependencies on other target modules).
